--TEST--
`Core\BigInt::toString` renders the digits `parse` reads back and keeps the sign at every width, and `echo` and string interpolation render through it — `rule:classes/stringable`, spec § 13
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\BigInt::toString
// The text is asserted by counting rather than read off one line: a member
// that lost the minus sign, or the top word of a wide value, renders
// plausibly on every small positive row. Each value is asked three ways — its
// own text, the number that text reads back to, and the text a string
// interpolation renders — and each count has to reach the number of values.
// The sweep carries both ends of the `int` range and a value no scalar holds,
// because the text is the one exact way out of this class at that width.
array<string> $values = [
    "0",
    "7",
    "-7",
    "9223372036854775807",
    "-9223372036854775808",
    "170141183460469231731687303715884105728",
    "-170141183460469231731687303715884105728",
];

int $seen = 0;
int $exact = 0;
int $readBack = 0;
int $rendered = 0;
foreach ($values as string $text) {
    var $value = Core\BigInt::parse($text);
    $seen = $seen + 1;
    if ($value->toString() == $text) {
        $exact = $exact + 1;
    }
    if (Core\BigInt::parse($value->toString())->compareTo($value) == 0) {
        $readBack = $readBack + 1;
    }
    if ("{$value}" == $text) {
        $rendered = $rendered + 1;
    }
}
echo $seen, " ", $exact, " ", $readBack, " ", $rendered, "\n";

// A number far wider than any scalar renders every one of its digits, and no
// separator is written between them.
echo Core\BigInt::of(1)->shl(200)->toString(), "\n";
--EXPECT--
7 7 7 7
1606938044258990275541962092341162602522202993782792835301376
